\section{Number \& Place Value}

\subsection*{Representing Numbers}
\VAR{ outcomes('representing_numbers') }

Every whole number is built from digits sitting in place-value columns (ones, tens, hundreds, thousands, \ldots). Each digit's value is \emph{digit $\times$ its column's value}.

\begin{stepsbox}
\begin{enumerate}[label=\arabic*.]
  \item Write the number's digits under column headings (Th, H, T, O, \ldots).
  \item Multiply each digit by its column value to get its \emph{expanded form}.
  \item To \textbf{round}: find the digit in the place you're rounding to, then look at the digit immediately to its right. 5 or more $\Rightarrow$ round up; less than 5 $\Rightarrow$ round down. Replace every digit to the right with 0.
\end{enumerate}
\end{stepsbox}

\begin{examplebox}{Expanded form of 4,637}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|c|}
\hline
Thousands & Hundreds & Tens & Ones \\
\hline
4 & 6 & 3 & 7 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
$4{,}637 = 4\times1000 + 6\times100 + 3\times10 + 7\times1 = 4000+600+30+7$
\end{examplebox}

\begin{examplebox}{Rounding 4,637 to the nearest hundred}
The hundreds digit is 6. The digit to its right (tens) is 3. Since $3 < 5$, round down: the hundreds digit stays 6 and everything after becomes 0. $\mathbf{4{,}637 \approx 4{,}600}$.
\end{examplebox}

\begin{examplebox}{15\% of \$80 (percentages, extension)}
Break the percentage into easy chunks: $10\%$ of \$80 is \$8. $5\%$ is half of $10\%$, so \$4. $15\% = 10\% + 5\% = \$8 + \$4 = \mathbf{\$12}$.
\end{examplebox}

