% ==============================================================================
% SECTION 2: COLUMN ARITHMETIC
% ==============================================================================
\section{Column Arithmetic}

All four column methods below share one rule: \textbf{line up the place values first} (ones under ones, tens under tens\ldots) before you calculate anything.

\subsection*{Vertical Addition}
\VAR{ outcomes('vertical_addition') }

\begin{stepsbox}
\begin{enumerate}[label=\arabic*.]
  \item Add the ones column first (rightmost).
  \item If the total is 10 or more, write the ones digit and \emph{carry} the ten into the next column.
  \item Add the tens column (plus any carry), then repeat leftwards.
\end{enumerate}
\end{stepsbox}

\begin{examplebox}{$347 + 289$}
\opadd{347}{289}
Ones: $7+9=16$, write 6, carry 1. Tens: $4+8+1=13$, write 3, carry 1. Hundreds: $3+2+1=6$. Answer: $\mathbf{636}$.
\end{examplebox}

\subsection*{Vertical Subtraction}
\VAR{ outcomes('vertical_subtraction') }

\begin{stepsbox}
\begin{enumerate}[label=\arabic*.]
  \item Subtract the ones column first.
  \item If the top digit is smaller than the bottom digit, \emph{borrow} a ten from the column to the left (adding 10 to the current column).
  \item If that column is a zero, keep borrowing further left until you find a column with something to lend.
\end{enumerate}
\end{stepsbox}

\begin{examplebox}{$702 - 458$ (borrowing across a zero)}
\opsub{702}{458}
Ones: $2-8$ won't work. The tens digit is 0, so borrow from the hundreds first: hundreds $7\to6$, tens becomes $10$. Now the tens can lend to the ones: tens $10\to9$, ones becomes $12$. Ones: $12-8=4$. Tens: $9-5=4$. Hundreds: $6-4=2$. Answer: $\mathbf{244}$.
\end{examplebox}

\begin{mistakebox}
Don't just "borrow a 1" and forget to reduce the column you borrowed \emph{from}. Every borrow takes 1 away from the column on the left and adds 10 to the column on the right.
\end{mistakebox}

\subsection*{Vertical Multiplication}
\VAR{ outcomes('vertical_multiplication') }

\begin{stepsbox}
\begin{enumerate}[label=\arabic*.]
  \item Multiply the top number by the ones digit of the bottom number, carrying as needed.
  \item If the bottom number has a tens digit too, multiply the top number by it, and shift that whole row one place left (this stands in for multiplying by a multiple of ten).
  \item Add the partial product rows together.
\end{enumerate}
\end{stepsbox}

\begin{examplebox}{$47 \times 6$ (single-digit multiplier)}
\opmul{47}{6}
$6\times7=42$, write 2, carry 4. $6\times4=24$, plus the carried 4 is 28. Answer: $\mathbf{282}$.
\end{examplebox}

\begin{examplebox}{$234 \times 23$ (two-digit multiplier, partial products)}
\opmul{234}{23}
Row 1: $234 \times 3 = 702$. Row 2: $234 \times 20 = 4{,}680$ --- written as $468$ shifted one place left. Add the rows: $702 + 4{,}680 = \mathbf{5{,}382}$.
\end{examplebox}

\subsection*{Long Division}
\VAR{ outcomes('long_division') }

This is the ``bus-stop'' method: divide, multiply, subtract, bring down --- then repeat.

\begin{stepsbox}
\begin{enumerate}[label=\arabic*.]
  \item Look at the first digit (or digits) of the dividend. How many times does the divisor fit? Write that above the bus stop.
  \item Multiply the divisor by that digit and subtract it from the part of the dividend you used.
  \item Bring down the next digit of the dividend and repeat.
  \item When there are no more digits to bring down, whatever is left over is the remainder.
\end{enumerate}
\end{stepsbox}

\begin{examplebox}{$144 \div 12$ (divides exactly)}
\opidiv{144}{12}
$14 \div 12 = 1$ remainder $2$; bring down the $4$ to make $24$. $24 \div 12 = 2$ remainder $0$. Answer: $\mathbf{12}$.
\end{examplebox}

\begin{examplebox}{$157 \div 4$ (with a remainder)}
\opidiv{157}{4}
$15\div4=3$ r$3$; bring down the $7$ to make $37$. $37\div4=9$ r$1$. Answer: $\mathbf{39}$ remainder $\mathbf{1}$.
\end{examplebox}

