% SECTION 3: FRACTIONS & DECIMALS
% ==============================================================================
\section{Fractions \& Decimals}

\subsection*{Fractions}
\VAR{ outcomes('fractions') }

A fraction is \emph{part of a whole}: $\dfrac{\text{numerator}}{\text{denominator}}$. The denominator says how many equal parts the whole is split into; the numerator says how many of those parts you have.

\begin{stepsbox}
\begin{enumerate}[label=\arabic*.]
  \item \textbf{Same denominator}: just add/subtract the numerators, keep the denominator.
  \item \textbf{Different denominators}: find a common denominator (a number both denominators divide into), convert both fractions, \emph{then} add/subtract.
  \item \textbf{Multiplying fractions}: multiply the numerators together and the denominators together, then simplify.
  \item \textbf{Mixed numbers}: convert to an improper fraction first (whole $\times$ denominator $+$ numerator), do the operation, then convert back.
  \item Always simplify your final answer by dividing numerator and denominator by their greatest common factor.
\end{enumerate}
\end{stepsbox}

\begin{examplebox}{Same denominator: $\frac{3}{8} + \frac{2}{8}$}
$\dfrac{3}{8} + \dfrac{2}{8} = \dfrac{3+2}{8} = \mathbf{\dfrac{5}{8}}$
\end{examplebox}

\begin{examplebox}{Different denominators: $\frac{1}{3} + \frac{1}{4}$}
Common denominator: 12. $\dfrac{1}{3} = \dfrac{4}{12}$, $\dfrac{1}{4} = \dfrac{3}{12}$. $\dfrac{4}{12} + \dfrac{3}{12} = \mathbf{\dfrac{7}{12}}$
\end{examplebox}

\begin{examplebox}{Multiplying: $\frac{2}{3} \times \frac{3}{4}$}
$\dfrac{2 \times 3}{3 \times 4} = \dfrac{6}{12}$. Simplify by dividing by 6: $\mathbf{\dfrac{1}{2}}$
\end{examplebox}

\begin{examplebox}{Mixed numbers: $2\frac{1}{2} + 1\frac{3}{4}$}
Convert: $2\frac{1}{2} = \dfrac{5}{2}$, $1\frac{3}{4} = \dfrac{7}{4}$. Common denominator 4: $\dfrac{5}{2}=\dfrac{10}{4}$. $\dfrac{10}{4}+\dfrac{7}{4} = \dfrac{17}{4} = \mathbf{4\dfrac{1}{4}}$
\end{examplebox}

