% SECTION 4: PATTERNS & ALGEBRA
% ==============================================================================
\section{Patterns \& Algebra}

\subsection*{Sequences}
\VAR{ outcomes('sequences') }

\begin{stepsbox}
\begin{enumerate}[label=\arabic*.]
  \item Compare consecutive terms. If they differ by the \emph{same amount} each time, it's \textbf{arithmetic} (add/subtract a constant).
  \item If each term is the \emph{same multiple} of the one before, it's \textbf{geometric} (multiply/divide by a constant).
  \item Once you know the rule, apply it to find the missing term.
\end{enumerate}
\end{stepsbox}

\begin{examplebox}{Arithmetic: $5, 9, 13, 17, \_\_, 25$}
Differences: $9-5=4$, $13-9=4$, $17-13=4$ --- the rule is ``$+4$''. Missing term: $17+4=\mathbf{21}$.
\end{examplebox}

\begin{examplebox}{Geometric: $3, 6, 12, 24, \_\_$}
Ratios: $6/3=2$, $12/6=2$, $24/12=2$ --- the rule is ``$\times 2$''. Missing term: $24\times2=\mathbf{48}$.
\end{examplebox}

\subsection*{Algebra}
\VAR{ outcomes('algebra') }

Solving for $x$ uses the \textbf{balance method}: whatever you do to one side of the equals sign, you must do to the other, so the equation stays true.

\begin{stepsbox}
\begin{enumerate}[label=\arabic*.]
  \item If there's a number added/subtracted with the $x$ term, undo it first (do the opposite to both sides).
  \item If $x$ is multiplied by a number, divide both sides by it last.
  \item If $x$ appears on both sides, move all the $x$ terms to one side first (by adding/subtracting), then solve as above.
\end{enumerate}
\end{stepsbox}

\begin{examplebox}{$x + 7 = 12$}
Subtract 7 from both sides: $x = 12 - 7 = \mathbf{5}$.
\end{examplebox}

\begin{examplebox}{$4x + 3 = 23$}
Subtract 3 from both sides: $4x = 20$. Divide both sides by 4: $x = \mathbf{5}$.
\end{examplebox}

\begin{examplebox}{$5x + 2 = 3x + 14$ (variables on both sides)}
Subtract $3x$ from both sides: $2x + 2 = 14$. Subtract 2: $2x = 12$. Divide by 2: $x = \mathbf{6}$. Check: $5(6)+2=32$ and $3(6)+14=32$ --- they match.
\end{examplebox}

