\section{Measurement \& Time}

\subsection*{Reading Time}
\VAR{ outcomes('reading_time') }

\begin{stepsbox}
\begin{enumerate}[label=\arabic*.]
  \item The \textbf{short} hand shows the hour --- read whichever hour it has \emph{just passed}.
  \item The \textbf{long} hand shows the minutes. Each number on the clock face is 5 minutes ($12\times5=60$).
  \item For elapsed time, add the hours and minutes of the duration separately, then carry 60 minutes over as 1 hour if needed.
\end{enumerate}
\end{stepsbox}

\begin{examplebox}{Half past 3}
\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]
  \draw[very thick] (0,0) circle (1.4cm);
  \foreach \x in {1,...,12} { \node[font=\small\bfseries] at (90-\x*30:1.15cm) {\x}; }
  \draw[line width=2pt,-stealth] (0,0) -- (0:0.65cm);
  \draw[line width=1.2pt,-stealth] (0,0) -- (90:1cm);
  \fill (0,0) circle (2pt);
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}
Hour hand between 3 and 4 (just passed 3); minute hand on 12 $\Rightarrow$ half past means 30 minutes. Time: $\mathbf{3{:}30}$.
\end{examplebox}

\begin{examplebox}{24-hour arrival time (elapsed time, extension)}
A flight departs at \textbf{19:30} and takes \textbf{5 hours 15 minutes}. Add hours: $19+5=24$. Add minutes: $30+15=45$. $24{:}45$ isn't valid, so subtract 24 hours and roll into the next day: $\mathbf{00{:}45}$ (next day).
\end{examplebox}

\subsection*{Date \& Calendar Duration}
\VAR{ outcomes('date_duration') }

Count forward from the start date to the end date. Watch for month boundaries --- months don't all have the same number of days.

\begin{examplebox}{From 20 May to 15 June}
Days left in May after the 20th: $31-20=11$. Add all of June up to the 15th: $+15$. Total: $11+15=\mathbf{26}$ days.
\end{examplebox}

\subsection*{Speed, Distance \& Time}
\VAR{ outcomes('speed_distance') }

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}
  \draw (0,0) -- (3,0) -- (1.5,2.2) -- cycle;
  \draw (0,0) -- (1.5,0.73);
  \node at (1.5,0.35) {Distance};
  \node at (0.55,1.15) {Speed};
  \node at (2.45,1.15) {Time};
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}
Cover the value you want to find: $\text{Distance} = \text{Speed} \times \text{Time}$, \ $\text{Speed} = \text{Distance} \div \text{Time}$, \ $\text{Time} = \text{Distance} \div \text{Speed}$.

\begin{examplebox}{$60$ km/h for 3 hours}
$\text{Distance} = \text{Speed}\times\text{Time} = 60\times3=\mathbf{180}$ km.
\end{examplebox}

\begin{examplebox}{Average speed over two legs (extension)}
Leg 1: 120 km in 2 h. Leg 2: 180 km in 3 h. Total distance $=300$ km. Total time $=5$ h. Average speed $= 300\div5=\mathbf{60}$ km/h.
\end{examplebox}

\begin{mistakebox}
Average speed is \emph{total} distance $\div$ \emph{total} time --- not the average of the two individual speeds (they only happen to agree here because the legs took different times).
\end{mistakebox}

