% SECTION 7: STATISTICS & PROBABILITY
% ==============================================================================
\section{Statistics \& Probability}

\subsection*{Data \& Graphing}
\VAR{ outcomes('data') }

For a category question (e.g. "which fruit was most popular?"), just compare the totals. For a numeric dataset:

\begin{stepsbox}
\begin{enumerate}[label=\arabic*.]
  \item \textbf{Mean} $=$ (sum of all values) $\div$ (number of values).
  \item \textbf{Median} $=$ the middle value once the data is sorted (average the middle two if there's an even number of values).
\end{enumerate}
\end{stepsbox}

\begin{examplebox}{Dataset: $12, 18, 22, 30, 38$}
Mean $= (12+18+22+30+38)\div5 = 120\div5=\mathbf{24}$. Median (the middle of 5 sorted values) $=\mathbf{22}$.
\end{examplebox}

\subsection*{Chance}
\VAR{ outcomes('chance') }

Likelihood in everyday words: impossible $\to$ unlikely $\to$ even chance $\to$ likely $\to$ certain. As a number, probability $=$ (favourable outcomes) $\div$ (total possible outcomes), written as a fraction.

\begin{examplebox}{Rolling an even number on a die}
Favourable outcomes: $\{2,4,6\}=3$. Total outcomes: 6. Probability $=\dfrac{3}{6}=\mathbf{\dfrac{1}{2}}$.
\end{examplebox}

\begin{examplebox}{Two coins: at least one Head}
All outcomes: $\{HH, HT, TH, TT\}$ --- 4 total. Favourable (at least one H): $\{HH,HT,TH\}$ --- 3. Probability $=\mathbf{\dfrac{3}{4}}$.
\end{examplebox}

